// REQUIRES: host-linux-amd64 || host-linux-arm64
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/run-golden.py --src %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_paths.gopp %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_paths.out
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/run-golden.py --src %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_maps.gopp %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_maps.out
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/run-golden.py --src %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_nested.gopp %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_nested.out
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/run-golden.py --src %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_borrow.gopp %S/Inputs/allocword/aw_borrow.out
// GOLDEN: by-hand
// P04.25, ADR-0029: an owned slice carries its allocator ({ptr, len, cap,
// allocator}, 32 bytes) and a map header stores its allocator. These programs
// use the test intrinsics (gopp_test_*, `gopp build -test-intrinsics`, the
// runlib builds a real binary for them): a slice or a map made around one of
// two counting allocators with the function table of P05 D5.3, and the call
// counters of both them and the global allocator. The programs print "ok" when
// every count is right:
//   aw_paths   make, literals, conversions, append on a nil slice (the global
//              allocator); append growth, Clone and the free routine (the
//              slice's own); copy, writes, re-slicing, views, unsafe.Slice (no
//              allocator at all);
//   aw_maps    make, literal and the first write to a nil map (global); a map
//              made in another allocator grows, is cleared, deleted from and
//              freed there; map-entry arguments copied in and out;
//   aw_nested  slices in struct fields, array elements, slice elements and
//              map values, and a map of maps, each with its own word;
//   aw_borrow  a borrowed []T, a view and a variadic call have no word; a
//              `move []T` parameter takes all four.
// The element-size tables are core-allocword-append.test. The programs are
// Go++ only (the intrinsics are not Go); the Go-defined behaviour they use is
// checked against `go run` by every other run test, which all still pass.
